 $\\mid c \\mid$?"] [17592186134238 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, B = {\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nConsider the subspace $V = Col(A)$ spanned by the columns $a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}$ of the matrix $A$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $Col(A)$, shown above. Given that $a_{1}⊥a_{2}$, and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{d}$?"] [17592186134243 :question/problem "$a_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, a_{2} = \\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, a_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -14\\end{bmatrix}, B = {[ \\mid; v_{1}; \\mid ], [ \\mid; v_{2}; \\mid ], \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nSuppose that $V = Span{a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}}$. Given that $B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$, and that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}$?"] [17592186134286 :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, B = {[ \\mid; v_{1}; \\mid ], [ \\mid; v_{2}; \\mid ], \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b \\\\ c \\\\ d\\end{bmatrix}}$\n\nConsider the subspace $V = Col(A)$ spanned by the columns $a_{1}, a_{2}, a_{3}$ of the matrix $A$, and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $Col(A)$, shown above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{d}{a}$?"] [17592186133526 :question/problem "Consider the following vectors:\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose that $V = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf a_3\\}$. Given that $\\mathcal B = \\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf b\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V, \\mathbf{a}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{a}_2$, and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{c}{a}$?"] [17592186133526 :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= a_{3} - proj_{v_{1}}a_{3} - proj_{v_{2}}a_{3} \\\\\n&= a_{3} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{2}}{v_{2} \\cdot v_{2}}v_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - (-\\frac{2}{4})\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{0}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - 0\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na=-\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\n$$\nc=-\\dfrac 3 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 1 2\\right)} = 3\n$$"] [17592186133600 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the subspace $V=\\textrm{Col}(A)$ spanned by the columns $\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,\\mathbf{a}_3$ of the matrix $A$, and an **orthonormal** basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$, shown above. Given that $\\mathbf{a}_1 \\perp \\mathbf{a}_2$, and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\mid \\,d\\, \\mid$?"] [17592186133600 :question/worked-solution "Since the first two vectors are already orthogonal, we proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2**: We set $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 3**. Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_3$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= a_{3} - proj_{v_{1}}a_{3} - proj_{v_{2}}a_{3} \\\\\n&= a_{3} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{2}}{v_{2} \\cdot v_{2}}v_{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf{v}_3$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{10}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{20}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize $\\mathbf{v}_3$ dividing it by\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf{v}_3\\| = \\sqrt{10}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{3} &= \\frac{v_{3}}{‖v_{3}‖} = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}\\,d\\, & = \\left\\end{vmatrix}-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\\right &= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{10}}{10}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186133527 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSuppose that $V = \\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf a_1, \\mathbf a_2, \\mathbf a_3\\}$. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is an orthogonal basis for $V$, and that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{b}{c}$?"] [17592186133527 :question/worked-solution "We find an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows:\n\n**Step 1.** We set $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n**Step 2.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1\\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{2} &= a_{2} - proj_{v_{1}}a_{2} \\\\\n&= a_{2} - \\frac{a_{2} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{3}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** We find the vector $\\mathbf{v}_3$ orthogonal to $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{v}_1,\\mathbf{v}_2 \\}$ using the usual formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv_{3} &= a_{3} - proj_{v_{1}}a_{3} - proj_{v_{2}}a_{3} \\\\\n&= a_{3} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{1}}{v_{1} \\cdot v_{1}}v_{1} - \\frac{a_{3} \\cdot v_{2}}{v_{2} \\cdot v_{2}}v_{2} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} - (-\\frac{3}{3})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{3}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(2\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} + 2\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nb=\\dfrac 3 2\n$$\n\n$$\nc=-\\dfrac 3 2\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{b}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}{\\left(-\\dfrac 3 2\\right)}=-1\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186131871 17592186131872 1759